          %p
            In other words, a composition law is an operation with the requisite shape to be the vertical composition in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            whose vertical arrows are algebras, whose horizontal arrows are arbitrary arrows, and whose 2-cells are commutative squares. Associativity is not assumed, but as noted below it often comes for free.
          %h2#relation_to_awfs Relation to awfs
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,R)
              is a functorial factorization whose underlying pointed endofunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi cod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cod
              has the structure of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{\mathbf{2}}
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cod
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,R)
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+weak+factorization+system"} algebraic weak factorization system
              if and only if it admits a right strong composition law.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              See
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Garner09"} Garner 09
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Garner10"} Garner 10
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Riehl11"} Riehl 11
              %a{:href => "#BR13"} Barthel-Riehl 13
              for proofs in varying degrees of explicitness.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,R)
              is a functorial factorization with a right weak composition law, and that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+free+monad"} algebraically free monad
              on the pointed endofunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              exists and is over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi cod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cod
              (for instance if it can be constructed by a
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+construction+of+free+algebras"} transfinite construction of free algebras
              = succeed "-pointed-endofunctor-algebras," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              By definition, the algebraically-free monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}(R)
              satisfies
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Alg
                      %mo =
                      %mi R
                      %mi Alg
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}(R) Alg = R Alg
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              extends to a strong one for
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}(R)
